      "id": "BHM-01",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-higher-rank-quadric-count",
      "title": "Higher-rank finite-field quadric point count, unit-length local densities, and nonzero-target valuation induction",
      "statement": "Let k be a finite field of odd characteristic and cardinality q, M ≥ 1 an integer, and B a nonsingular symmetric 2M × 2M matrix over k. For every nonzero s ∈ k^×, the quadratic level set Q(x) = ½ xᵀ B x = s in k^(2M) has cardinality N(s) satisfying |N(s) − q^(2M−1)| ≤ q^(M−1) (with equality). Consequently, at any finite place v of a number field F with |2|_v = 1, residue cardinality q_v, standard additive Haar measure λ_v (λ_v(𝒪_v) = 1), and unimodular integral symmetric matrix B ∈ M_(2M)(𝒪_v), every local unit s ∈ 𝒪_v^× admits a constant density D ≥ 0 with |D − 1| ≤ 2 / q_v^M such that λ_v^(2M)({x ∈ 𝒪_v^(2M) : Q(x) ∈ A}) = D · λ_v(A) for every measurable A ⊆ {z ∈ F_v : |z − s|_v < |s|_v}. Applying this to the trace form of a nondegenerate Hermitian matrix J of rank M ≥ 1 over a CM extension E/F yields, at all but finitely many finite places v, the local Gram-fibre unit estimate |m_v(s)(Λ_v) − 1| ≤ 2 / q_v^M on the standard lattice Λ_v ⊂ (F_v ⊗_F E)^(1 × M), and in particular |m_v(s)(Λ_v) − 1| ≤ 2 / q_v² at M = 2. Moreover, for every higher rank M ≥ 3 and every nonzero integral target s ∈ 𝒪_v ∖ {0}, strong induction on ord_v(s) in steps of two yields a constant local pushforward density D ≥ 0 on {z ∈ F_v : |z − s|_v < |s|_v} satisfying |D − 1| ≤ 2 / q_v^(M−1).",
      "hypotheses": "Odd-characteristic finite field k of cardinality q; number field F (and CM extension E/F with conjugation σ and F-basis b) with finite place v of residue cardinality q_v and uniformizer π; standard additive Haar measure λ_v on F_v with λ_v(𝒪_v) = 1; unimodular integral symmetric 2M × 2M matrix B over 𝒪_v (with |2|_v = 1 and |det B|_v = 1), or nondegenerate M × M Hermitian matrix J over E; rank M ≥ 1 for unit targets s ∈ 𝒪_v^× and M ≥ 3 for arbitrary nonzero integral targets s ∈ 𝒪_v ∖ {0}.",
      "correspondence": "Orthogonal diagonalization splits the 2M-variable quadratic Gauss sum over k into 2M one-variable quadratic character sums whose χ(t)-scaling cancels via χ(t)^(2M) = 1, giving sharp square-root cancellation on nonzero targets. Hensel lifting over primitive residue classes transfers the count to the local pushforward density on unit balls, and for M ≥ 3 separating primitive vectors from π 𝒪_v^(2M) yields the scaling recursion D = D_p + q_v^(−(2M−2)) D' preserving |D − 1| ≤ 2 / q_v^(M−1) across every two-step valuation descent.",
      "dependencies": [],
