    {
      "id": "BHM-02",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-global-product-compatibility",
      "title": "Finite-place orbit factorization, higher-rank Euler convergence, block induction, powerset ideal sieve, and complex Euler integrability",
      "statement": "Let E/F be a CM extension over a totally real number field F with F-basis b, J a nondegenerate M × M Hermitian matrix over E, and T an invertible n × n Hermitian matrix over E. (1) Any locally finite U(J, 𝔸_F)-invariant measure I on (𝔸_F ⊗_F E)^(n × M) supported on {X : gram(X) = T} restricts on the off-S lattice locus {X : X_v ∈ Λ_v for all v ∉ S} and pushes forward to W_∞ × ∏_(v ∈ S) W_v as a nonnegative scalar multiple c_S · (m_∞(T) × ∏_(v ∈ S) m_v(T)) of the local Gram-fibre product; the same product factorization extends to open invariant regular subsets via open-embedding pullback. (2) Whenever n = 0 or n + 2 ≤ M, splitting an anisotropic vector T ≃ diag(t, T₁) and inducting on n produces an explicit constant C_n = 3^n − 1 such that |m_v(T)(Λ_v) − 1| ≤ C_n / q_v² for all but finitely many places v, while m_v(T)(Λ_v) < ∞ for every v, whence ∑_v |m_v(T)(Λ_v) − 1| < ∞. (3) For n + 2 ≤ M and t ∈ F^×, the global Siegel–Weil measure identity for (J, T) implies the identity for (diag(t, J), diag(t, T)) with global constant 1 via product-formula cancellation c_∞ ∏_(v ∈ S) c_v = 1 and convergent tail multiplication; for n + 3 ≤ M, if t is not globally represented by J, vanishing of some local rank-one fibre propagates to the block-extended fibre and gives the global Siegel–Weil identity 0 = 0. (4) For any finite set D of primes disjoint from S and any scaled rational sample B ⊂ F^× with common integer denominator N, powerset expansion over t ⊆ D and product-ideal divisibility yield δ^[F:ℚ] ∑_(x ∈ B) ∏_(v ∈ D) |g_v(x) − 1| ≤ max(C₀, 0) ∏_(v ∈ D) (2 C / q_v²). Finally, for any constant-one Euler-product measure I with locally finite factors, a continuous m_∞-integrable archimedean function Φ_∞, and a pure finite-adelic test Φ_f = ∏_v Φ_v, the product Φ = Φ_∞ Φ_f is I-integrable with ∫ Φ d I = (∫ Φ_∞ dm_∞) · ∏_v (∫ Φ_v dm_v).",
      "hypotheses": "CM extension E/F with F totally real and F-basis b, nondegenerate M × M Hermitian matrix J over E, invertible n × n Hermitian matrix T over E, standard local additive Haar measures on A_∞ and each F_v with associated local Gram-fibre families m_∞ and m_v, convergence range n = 0 or n + 2 ≤ M (and n + 3 ≤ M for anisotropic vanishing), nonzero scalar t ∈ F^×, ideal-lattice counting bound with constant C₀ and scaling parameter δ > 0 on compact archimedean sets K ⊂ A_∞, and a factorizable test Φ = Φ_∞ Φ_f with Φ_∞ continuous and m_∞-integrable.",
      "correspondence": "Proper-action orbit uniqueness on closed orbits and open regular embeddings supplies finite-place product factorization of unitary-invariant measures. Anisotropic splitting reduces higher-rank local deviations inductively via the rank-one M ≥ 3 bound of BHM-01 and the quadratic error rule (3|C| + 2)/q², proving absolute convergence of the Euler product. Rank-one block induction unfolds the theta measure and cancels local Haar scaling factors by the global product formula, while powerset expansion over subsets t ⊆ D couples the unit and nonunit local bounds to ideal-norm counts and box-measure evaluation extends Euler factorization to integrable non-compactly-supported archimedean functions.",
      "dependencies": [
        "BHM-01",
        "ODN-02"
      ],
